<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Jong Hoon Shin, his work, education, and publications."
>
  <section class="about-page">
    <header class="about-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">About Jong Hoon Shin</p>
      <p class="lead">
        Hi! I’m Jong Hoon Shin. I started out trying to understand the world
        through physics, mathematics, electrical engineering, and computer
        science—and ended up building technology that tries to make it better.
        These days, I work in computer architecture, artificial intelligence,
        hardware acceleration, software–hardware co-design, and performance
        modeling. I love learning new things, organizing what I learn, and
        turning it into something useful. I used to dream about people like
        Richard Feynman, John Bardeen, and John von Neumann; now I’m trying to
        live a version of that dream through silicon and code. I’m always up
        for reading a good book, solving a deep problem, or sharing a fresh
        idea.
      </p>
      <ul class="expertise-list" aria-label="Areas of expertise">
        <li>AI accelerators</li>
        <li>Performance modeling</li>
        <li>RISC-V CPUs</li>
        <li>Memory systems</li>
        <li>HW–SW co-design</li>
        <li>RRAM computing</li>
      </ul>
      <nav class="profile-links" aria-label="Professional profiles">
        <a
          href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=rgJp-dYAAAAJ&hl=en"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Google Scholar ↗</a>
        <a
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jong-hoon-shin-b98b8673"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >LinkedIn ↗</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="career" aria-labelledby="career-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Work & education</p>
      <h2 id="career-heading">From materials to machines</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <time>Jul 2025—Now</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>Machine Learning ASIC Engineer</h3>
            <p class="career-place">Meta · Sunnyvale, California</p>
            <p>
              I work on Meta’s Infra Silicon Architecture team, building
              performance models for next-generation accelerators across the
              company’s AI workloads. This work sits alongside Meta’s broader
              investment in vertically integrated systems such as MTIA, where
              custom silicon and software are designed together for large-scale
              inference, ranking, and recommendation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <time>Jul 2019—May 2025</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>Staff Hardware Engineer</h3>
            <p class="career-place">Samsung Semiconductor · San Jose, California</p>
            <p>
              I modeled and optimized systems ranging from RISC-V processors to
              deep-learning accelerators and distributed AI hardware. Projects
              included a cycle-accurate out-of-order CPU model, DAG-based
              simulation of distributed training, SystemC analysis of SoC
              memory systems, and analytical accelerator models exploring
              dataflow, hierarchy, interconnect, mixed precision, sparsity, and
              compression.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <time>Sep 2014—May 2019</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering</h3>
            <p class="career-place">University of Michigan · Ann Arbor, Michigan</p>
            <p>
              My doctoral research explored computing beyond conventional
              von Neumann systems with resistive memory. I implemented
              RRAM-based accelerators for stochastic neural-network learning
              and spin-glass simulated annealing, demonstrated online feature
              extraction with analog tantalum-oxide arrays and Oja’s rule, and
              helped develop low-power Cu-based RRAM devices for neuromorphic
              and in-memory computing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <time>Jul 2011—Jun 2014</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>Research Engineer</h3>
            <p class="career-place">LG Electronics · Seoul, South Korea</p>
            <p>
              I researched energy-efficient power electronics using III–V
              compound semiconductors, including GaAs and InGaAs/AlGaAs
              transistors for automotive applications. TCAD studies of planar
              and gate-all-around nanowire FETs connected device physics,
              short-channel behavior, and subthreshold optimization to
              practical low-power, high-frequency design.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <time>Mar 2008—Feb 2010</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>M.S. in Physics</h3>
            <p class="career-place">Seoul National University · Seoul, South Korea</p>
            <p>
              Advised by Prof. Tae Won Noh, I studied superconducting materials
              through optical spectroscopy. The work joined quantum mechanics,
              optics, electromagnetism, and solid-state physics with
              experimental planning, measurement, and analysis of real
              materials.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <time>Mar 2002—Feb 2008</time>
          <div class="career-detail">
            <h3>B.S. in Physics</h3>
            <p class="career-place">Seoul National University · Seoul, South Korea</p>
            <p>
              I built a foundation in physics and mathematics while also
              studying computer science, circuits, optics, and solid-state
              physics. A philosophy and social-science reading club called
              “Small College,” along with teaching-assistant work, helped shape
              my interest in how science and technology affect society.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="publications" aria-labelledby="publications-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Selected record</p>
      <h2 id="publications-heading">Publications</h2>
      <ol>
        {publications.map((publication) => (
          <li>
            <a
              href={publication.href ?? scholarSearch(publication.title)}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >{publication.title}</a>
            <small>{publication.venue}</small>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
